<article class="rag-story simple-view" aria-label="An interactive introduction to retrieval augmented generation">
  <header class="story-hero">
    <span class="story-kicker">FIELD NOTE · RAG SYSTEMS</span>
    <h2>How an AI system answers using the right evidence.</h2>
    <p class="story-dek">RAG does not teach a model new facts. It searches a separate knowledge base, places relevant excerpts next to the question, and asks the model to answer from them.</p>
    <div class="story-note"><span>Reading time · 6 minutes</span><span>Simulated fixture · optional browser-local model</span></div>
    <div class="view-toggle" role="group" aria-label="Choose explanation depth"><span>View</span><button class="view-button active" data-view="simple" type="button">Simple</button><button class="view-button" data-view="engineering" type="button">Engineering</button></div>
  </header>
  <div class="story-body">
    <nav class="story-progress" aria-label="RAG walkthrough sections"><span class="progress-label">ON THIS PAGE</span><a href="#rag-problem">Problem</a><a href="#rag-index">Indexing</a><a href="#rag-query">Retrieval</a><a href="#rag-grounding">Grounding</a><a href="#rag-quality">Evaluation</a></nav>
    <div class="story-content">

  <section class="story-section opening-section" id="rag-problem">
    <div class="section-number">01</div>
    <div class="section-copy"><span class="story-kicker">THE PROBLEM</span><h3>A language model can sound certain about information it does not have.</h3><p>Imagine asking an assistant about a company policy written yesterday. The model may know how to write an answer, but it cannot reliably know that private or recently changed policy unless the system gives it the relevant source.</p></div>
    <div class="problem-demo"><div class="problem-column"><span class="mini-label">WITHOUT RETRIEVAL</span><p>“What happens when a payment exception is unresolved?”</p><div class="answer bad">I think it is escalated according to the usual process…<b>Confidence without evidence.</b></div></div><div class="problem-arrow">→</div><div class="problem-column"><span class="mini-label">WITH RETRIEVAL</span><p>“What happens when a payment exception is unresolved?”</p><div class="answer good">After the first review, route it to the regional operations queue and record a reason code.<b>Answer + source pointer.</b></div></div></div>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section core-section">
    <div class="section-number">02</div>
    <div class="section-copy"><span class="story-kicker">THE CORE IDEA</span><h3>RAG is three moves: retrieve, augment, generate.</h3><p>First, find relevant passages. Then add those passages to the model’s input. Finally, generate an answer that stays within the evidence you supplied.</p></div>
    <div class="core-diagram"><div class="core-card"><span>01</span><b>Retrieve</b><small>Find relevant passages</small></div><i>+</i><div class="core-card"><span>02</span><b>Augment</b><small>Add passages to the prompt</small></div><i>+</i><div class="core-card"><span>03</span><b>Generate</b><small>Answer with citations</small></div></div>
    <div class="takeaway">RAG does not retrain the model. It gives the model better material to work with at answer time.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="guided-lab" aria-label="Guided RAG question walkthrough">
    <div class="guided-heading"><span class="story-kicker">START HERE · GUIDED MODE</span><h3>Run one question through the whole system.</h3><p>Follow one request from words to evidence to answer. You can stop at any stage and inspect what changed.</p><button class="guided-start" data-guide-start type="button">Run this question →</button></div>
    <div class="guided-question"><span class="mini-label">USER QUESTION</span><b>“What happens when a payment exception is unresolved?”</b></div>
    <div class="guided-layout"><div class="guided-steps"><button class="guided-step active" data-guide-step="0"><span>01</span><b>Question</b><small>What the user asked</small></button><button class="guided-step" data-guide-step="1"><span>02</span><b>Rewrite</b><small>Clarify intent</small></button><button class="guided-step" data-guide-step="2"><span>03</span><b>Retrieve</b><small>Find candidates</small></button><button class="guided-step" data-guide-step="3"><span>04</span><b>Filter</b><small>Check access</small></button><button class="guided-step" data-guide-step="4"><span>05</span><b>Rerank</b><small>Choose evidence</small></button><button class="guided-step" data-guide-step="5"><span>06</span><b>Augment</b><small>Pack context</small></button><button class="guided-step" data-guide-step="6"><span>07</span><b>Generate</b><small>Write response</small></button></div><div class="guided-detail"><span class="story-kicker" data-guide-kicker>THE INPUT</span><h4 data-guide-title>Start with what the user actually asked.</h4><p data-guide-copy>The request is natural language. It is meaningful to a person, but the system still needs to turn it into a search intent.</p><div class="guided-artifact" data-guide-artifact>payment exception · unresolved?</div><div class="guided-footer"><span data-guide-count>Step 1 of 7</span><button class="guided-next" data-guide-next type="button">Next step →</button></div></div></div>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section index-section" id="rag-index">
    <div class="section-number">03</div>
    <div class="section-copy"><span class="story-kicker">BEFORE A QUESTION ARRIVES</span><h3>First, make the knowledge searchable.</h3><p>This is the indexing path: prepare documents before anyone asks a question. It usually runs when documents are added or refreshed, not every time someone asks.</p></div>
    <div class="index-story"><div class="index-steps"><button class="index-step active" data-index-step="ingest"><span>1</span><b>Ingest</b><small>Parse files and preserve their source.</small></button><button class="index-step" data-index-step="chunk"><span>2</span><b>Chunk</b><small>Split documents into complete ideas.</small></button><button class="index-step" data-index-step="embed"><span>3</span><b>Embed</b><small>Represent meaning as vectors.</small></button><button class="index-step" data-index-step="store"><span>4</span><b>Index</b><small>Store vectors, terms, and metadata.</small></button></div><div class="index-explanation"><span class="story-kicker" data-index-kicker>INGEST</span><h4 data-index-title>Keep the source attached to the text.</h4><p data-index-copy>Documents can come from object storage, APIs, databases, wikis, or uploads. Parsing should preserve headings, tables, timestamps, and a pointer to the original source.</p><div class="index-visual" data-index-visual><span class="doc-badge">PDF</span><b>Payments operations handbook</b><small>42 pages · Finance / Analyst · updated 2 days ago</small></div></div></div>
    <div class="chunk-lab"><div><span class="story-kicker">SMALL EXPERIMENT</span><h4>Chunk size and overlap shape what retrieval can see.</h4><p>A chunk is a searchable excerpt from a larger document. Adjust the controls below and watch the same text become smaller, larger, or more connected across boundaries.</p></div></div>
    <div class="chunk-input-lab"><div><span class="story-kicker">TRY A DOCUMENT</span><h4>See the boundaries move.</h4><p>Use the sample or paste your own text. The preview uses the chunk size and overlap sliders below, so you can see how much context each chunk keeps.</p></div><div class="chunk-slider-controls"><label class="chunk-slider">Chunk size <output data-chunk-size-output>240 tokens</output><input type="range" min="180" max="900" step="60" value="240" data-chunk-size /></label><label class="chunk-slider">Overlap <output data-chunk-overlap-output>20 tokens</output><input type="range" min="0" max="120" step="10" value="20" data-chunk-overlap /></label><p class="chunk-slider-note" data-chunk-slider-note>Balanced starting point: enough context for the rule without adding much unrelated text.</p></div><label class="chunk-input">Text to chunk<textarea data-chunk-text rows="6">A payment exception is first reviewed by the operations analyst. The analyst checks the transaction history, verifies the reason code, and records the initial decision. If it remains unresolved after the first review, route it to the regional operations queue and record the reason code.&#10;&#10;The queue owner reviews the exception within one business day. The owner may request more information or escalate it to the regional lead when the reason code is missing. Every escalation should preserve the original evidence, the reviewer notes, and the timestamp of the decision.&#10;&#10;When the case is resolved, the owner updates the queue status and links the final decision to the exception record. This makes the process auditable and gives future reviewers enough context to understand what happened.</textarea></label><div class="chunk-preview" data-chunk-preview aria-live="polite"></div></div>
    <div class="concept-footnote"><b>In practice:</b> embeddings enable semantic search; sparse indexes preserve exact terms and identifiers; metadata enables filtering by source, tenant, freshness, or access scope. PostgreSQL with pgvector is one way to keep vectors close to application data.</div>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section query-section" id="rag-query">
    <div class="section-number">04</div>
    <div class="section-copy"><span class="story-kicker">WHEN A QUESTION ARRIVES</span><h3>Search is a small pipeline, not a single similarity lookup.</h3><p>The system first clarifies the question, finds several possible excerpts, removes anything the user is not allowed to see, and then selects the evidence most likely to support an answer.</p></div>
    <div class="query-story"><div class="query-timeline"><button class="query-step active" data-query-step="rewrite"><b>01</b><span>Rewrite</span><small>Clarify intent</small></button><button class="query-step" data-query-step="retrieve"><b>02</b><span>Retrieve</span><small>Dense + sparse search</small></button><button class="query-step" data-query-step="filter"><b>03</b><span>Filter</span><small>Apply permissions</small></button><button class="query-step" data-query-step="rerank"><b>04</b><span>Rerank</span><small>Order evidence</small></button><button class="query-step" data-query-step="answer"><b>05</b><span>Answer</span><small>Cite or abstain</small></button></div><div class="query-explanation"><span class="story-kicker" data-query-kicker>QUERY REWRITE</span><h4 data-query-title>Make the user’s wording searchable.</h4><p data-query-copy>“What happens when a payment exception is unresolved?” becomes a clearer retrieval intent: payment exception, unresolved, escalation rule.</p><div class="query-signal" data-query-signal>payment exception · unresolved · escalation rule</div></div></div>
    <div class="retrieval-lab"><div class="lab-heading"><div><span class="story-kicker">SMALL EXPERIMENT</span><h4>Why combine retrieval methods?</h4><p class="lab-subtitle">Semantic search looks for meaning. Keyword search looks for exact words. Hybrid search combines both; reranking reorders the best candidates with the question in view.</p></div><div class="retrieval-tabs"><button class="retrieval-tab active" data-retrieval="hybrid">Hybrid</button><button class="retrieval-tab" data-retrieval="semantic">Semantic</button><button class="retrieval-tab" data-retrieval="keyword">Keyword</button><button class="retrieval-tab" data-retrieval="reranked">Reranked</button></div></div><div class="retrieval-table"><div class="retrieval-row best" data-retrieval-row="policy"><span>01</span><div><b>Escalation policy · section 4.2</b><small>Exact rule and matching intent · Finance / Analyst</small></div><strong>0.94</strong></div><div class="retrieval-row" data-retrieval-row="checklist"><span>02</span><div><b>Exception review checklist</b><small>Related process, weaker answer signal</small></div><strong>0.71</strong></div><div class="retrieval-row restricted" data-retrieval-row="ownership"><span>03</span><div><b>Regional queue ownership notes</b><small>Relevant wording, blocked by access filter</small></div><strong>0.68</strong></div></div><p class="lab-note" data-retrieval-note>Hybrid retrieval catches both meaning and exact business language. Reranking then spends more compute on a smaller, stronger set. Scores are illustrative fixture values.</p></div>
    <section class="local-sandbox" aria-label="Local pasted text retrieval sandbox">
      <div class="sandbox-heading"><span class="story-kicker">TRY YOUR OWN TEXT · LOCAL ONLY</span><h3>See retrieval work on something you provide.</h3><p>Paste a few paragraphs, ask a question, and compare exact keyword matching with browser-based semantic search. Your text stays in this browser; only the embedding model is downloaded and cached on first use.</p></div>
      <div class="privacy-badge">✓ Your text stays in this browser</div>
      <div class="sandbox-inputs"><label>Knowledge text<textarea data-local-text rows="8" placeholder="Paste a policy, article, notes, or any text you want to search…"></textarea></label><label>Question<input data-local-query value="What happens when a payment exception is unresolved?" /></label><div class="sandbox-actions"><button class="sandbox-run" data-local-run type="button">Keyword retrieval →</button><button class="sandbox-run semantic-run" data-semantic-run type="button">Load model &amp; search semantically →</button></div></div>
      <div class="sandbox-status" data-local-status>Waiting for text. Add your own content to create local chunks.</div>
      <div class="sandbox-model-note">Semantic mode demonstrates embedding retrieval only: no query rewriting, hybrid search, access filtering, reranking, or answer generation.</div>
      <div class="sandbox-results" data-local-results hidden><div class="sandbox-summary"><span><b data-local-count>0</b> chunks created</span><span><b data-local-matches>0</b> matches found</span><span><b data-local-method>keyword</b> retrieval method</span></div><div class="local-chunk-list" data-local-chunks></div><div class="sandbox-model-note">Similarity scores are relative ranking signals, not answer confidence.</div></div>
    </section>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section answer-section" id="rag-grounding">
    <div class="section-number">05</div>
    <div class="section-copy"><span class="story-kicker">THE EVIDENCE BOUNDARY</span><h3>The model should see a small, relevant context—not the whole database.</h3><p>After ranking, the system packs the best excerpts into a context window: the part of the model’s input reserved for the question and supporting evidence. Source pointers travel with the excerpts so the answer can be checked.</p></div>
    <div class="answer-demo"><div class="answer-text"><span class="story-kicker">GROUNDED RESPONSE</span><p>After the first review, an unresolved payment exception is routed to the regional operations queue and recorded with a reason code.</p></div><div class="citation"><b>Source</b><span>Payments operations handbook · §4.2</span><em>Supported by retained evidence</em></div></div>
    <div class="context-lab"><div><span class="story-kicker">SMALL EXPERIMENT</span><h4>More context is not always better context.</h4><p>Context assembly means choosing which retrieved excerpts to send to the model. It balances answer quality, response time, and token cost. Choose a strategy to see the evidence boundary change.</p></div><div class="context-controls"><button class="context-button active" data-context="focused">Focused</button><button class="context-button" data-context="wide">Wide</button><button class="context-button" data-context="unsupported">Unsupported</button><div class="context-result active" data-context-result="focused"><div class="context-window"><i class="context-hit"></i><i class="context-hit"></i><i></i><i></i></div><b>Answer with §4.2 and one supporting passage.</b><small>Low noise · clear citation · lower token cost</small></div></div></div>
    <div class="takeaway">When evidence is weak, the most useful answer may be: “I could not find enough support for that. Can you narrow the question or provide another source?”</div>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section quality-section" id="rag-quality">
    <div class="section-number">06</div>
    <div class="section-copy"><span class="story-kicker">THE QUALITY LOOP</span><h3>RAG is only useful when the whole path is measured.</h3><p>A fluent answer can still be wrong. Teams evaluate two separate questions: did retrieval find the right evidence, and did generation use that evidence faithfully? They also watch latency, cost, freshness, access decisions, and traces in production.</p></div>
    <div class="metrics"><div><b>Recall@5</b><strong>0.92</strong><small>Did we retrieve the relevant chunk?</small></div><div><b>Context precision</b><strong>0.86</strong><small>How much retrieved context was useful?</small></div><div><b>Faithfulness</b><strong>0.96</strong><small>Did the answer follow its evidence?</small></div><div><b>Relevancy</b><strong>0.89</strong><small>Did it answer the question?</small></div><p class="metrics-note">Illustrative fixture metrics only. A real evaluation set would compare many questions and expected evidence.</p></div>
    <div class="failure-lab"><div><span class="story-kicker">FAILURE MODE LAB</span><h4>What should the system do when things go wrong?</h4><div class="failure-buttons"><button class="failure-button active" data-failure="miss">Missed evidence</button><button class="failure-button" data-failure="stale">Stale source</button><button class="failure-button" data-failure="access">Access conflict</button><button class="failure-button" data-failure="unsupported">Unsupported claim</button></div></div><div class="failure-result"><b data-failure-title>Recall fell because the relevant chunk never entered the candidate set.</b><p data-failure-copy>Improve parsing, query rewriting, chunk boundaries, or retrieval coverage. Do not ask the generator to guess.</p><span data-failure-action>Next move: inspect retrieval recall and add a representative test question.</span></div></div>
    <div class="production-pattern"><div><span class="story-kicker">PRODUCTION PATTERN</span><h4>What this looks like beyond the demo.</h4><p>A reliable RAG service connects retrieval quality to software engineering and observability. The implementation details below are representative patterns from my applied AI work—not client data.</p></div><div class="pattern-grid"><span><b>Data</b>Object storage · ingestion jobs · PostgreSQL · pgvector</span><span><b>Retrieval</b>Semantic + keyword search · RRF · reranking · access filters</span><span><b>Quality</b>DeepEval · hallucination checks · golden questions · faithfulness</span><span><b>Operations</b>LangSmith tracing · latency alerts · caching · retry/fallback paths</span><span><b>Service</b>FastAPI · Pydantic · pytest · structured outputs · OpenAPI</span><span><b>Runtime</b>Docker · Kubernetes · secrets · authentication · production support</span></div></div>
  </section>

  <section class="glossary-section"><span class="story-kicker">QUICK GLOSSARY</span><h3>Words you will see in RAG discussions.</h3><div class="term-legend"><span class="term" data-tip="A numerical representation of meaning used for similarity search.">Embedding</span><span class="term" data-tip="Search that combines vector meaning with exact keyword matching.">Hybrid search</span><span class="term" data-tip="A second relevance pass that reorders retrieved passages.">Reranking</span><span class="term" data-tip="Whether an answer is supported by its retrieved evidence.">Faithfulness</span><span class="term" data-tip="The bounded input area containing the question and evidence.">Context window</span><span class="term" data-tip="A deliberate response that says the evidence is insufficient.">Abstention</span></div><div class="glossary-grid"><details><summary>Embedding</summary><p>A numerical representation of meaning used for similarity search.</p></details><details><summary>Hybrid search</summary><p>Combining semantic vector retrieval with keyword or sparse retrieval.</p></details><details><summary>Reranking</summary><p>A second, more query-aware relevance pass over a smaller candidate set.</p></details><details><summary>Faithfulness</summary><p>Whether the generated answer is supported by the retrieved context.</p></details><details><summary>Context window</summary><p>The bounded input space where the model receives the question and evidence.</p></details><details><summary>Abstention</summary><p>A deliberate “not enough evidence” response instead of unsupported confidence.</p></details></div></section>

  <section class="knowledge-check"><span class="story-kicker">QUICK CHECK</span><h3>Can you predict what the system should do?</h3><p>Use the page’s concepts once before you leave. These are not scored publicly; they are here to make the mental model stick.</p><div class="check-question" data-check-question="0"><b>1 / 3 · What does an embedding help with?</b><div class="check-options"><button data-check-answer="wrong">Writing the final answer</button><button data-check-answer="correct">Finding related meaning</button><button data-check-answer="wrong">Checking user permissions</button></div><div class="check-feedback" data-check-feedback>Choose an answer.</div></div><div class="check-question hidden" data-check-question="1"><b>2 / 3 · When should a system abstain?</b><div class="check-options"><button data-check-answer="correct">When retained evidence cannot support the claim</button><button data-check-answer="wrong">Whenever a query has more than five words</button><button data-check-answer="wrong">Only when the model is offline</button></div><div class="check-feedback" data-check-feedback>Choose an answer.</div></div><div class="check-question hidden" data-check-question="2"><b>3 / 3 · Why apply access filtering before generation?</b><div class="check-options"><button data-check-answer="wrong">To make the model answer faster only</button><button data-check-answer="wrong">To replace retrieval evaluation</button><button data-check-answer="correct">To keep unauthorised evidence out of context</button></div><div class="check-feedback" data-check-feedback>Choose an answer.</div></div><button class="check-next" data-check-next type="button" disabled>Next question →</button></section>

    </div>
  </div>
</article>

